

🜁 1. Evaluate Regime — “What mode are we in?”

The orchestrator reads the lattice:

warp intensity

gravity gradient

stress load

charge flux

Then compares them to the organism’s thresholds.

Example:

if stress < 0.4 and warp < 0.6 → stable
if stress in [0.4, 0.8] → critical
if warp >= 0.8 → exotic
if stress > 0.9 → failure

This is the diagnosis step.

🜂 2. Apply Regime Effects — “How should we behave now?”

Once the regime is known, the orchestrator modifies behavior:

stable: full performance

critical: clamp outputs, reduce warp, stabilize

exotic: allow high‑warp, unusual physics

failure: shut down, collapse fields, trigger crash analyzer

This is the response step.

🜃 3. Transition Rules — “When do we switch modes?”

The orchestrator enforces:

hysteresis (to prevent rapid switching)

thresholds (defined in the DSL)

safety envelopes

escalation logic

Example:

You can’t jump from stable → exotic directly.

You must pass through critical.

Failure overrides everything.

This is the governance step.

